# Meltdown — Game state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely.

The one contract fixed over the state is the debugging and automation surface
specified for this build. Every operation that surface carries is a read or a
pose of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read, honors
what they write, and returns to its title-screen values when the surface's
`reset` is called. Beyond satisfying that surface, how the state is organized is
yours to decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following.

## The screen and the run

- The screen the game is showing, one of `title`, `modeselect`,
  `difficultyselect`, `howto`, `playing`, `paused`, `victory`, and `gameover`.
- The sub-phase of the `playing` screen, one of `opening`, `building`, and
  `wave`.
- The row highlighted on whichever menu the current screen shows, counted from
  `0`.
- The mode and the difficulty the run is being played on.
- The money, the lives remaining, the score, and the current wave number.
- The seconds left on the build timer, and the units of the current wave still to
  be released.
- The wave spawner's own release clock.
- The game speed, `1` or `2`.

## The floor

- Every tower standing on the floor, each with a distinct id, its type, its
  footprint's top-left tile, its placement rotation, its level, its heat, whether
  it is tripped and the seconds left on that cooldown, its own fire accumulator,
  the id of the unit it is firing on, whether it is firing this frame, its kill
  and damage tallies, everything spent on it, whether it is still fresh, and the
  two faculty gates the debug surface poses.
- Every surge unit on the floor, each with a distinct id, its type, its centre,
  its hp and maximum hp, its live slow factor and the seconds that slow has left,
  the vent it entered at, and the motion gate the debug surface poses.
- The tower selected, or none; the shop entry hovered, or none; and the held
  build preview with its type, its footprint's top-left tile, and its rotation,
  or none.

## The rest

- Whether the run's own release of surge is running, which the debug surface
  poses. It is on when a run begins.
- The pointer's position in logical stage units and whether it is pressed,
  refreshed in every update.
- Whether sound is muted, refreshed from the runtime's mute bit in every update.
- The simulation time, in seconds, which every update adds the game time it
  advanced by, whatever the screen.
- The state of the seeded random generator, which the vent each unit enters at is
  drawn from.
- Whatever the game needs to hand a fresh id to the next tower or unit it
  creates, so no two entities live at one moment share an id.

Everything the mode and difficulty derive, the routes across the floor, and the
rectangles the build panel laid its controls out on and the current screen drew
its menu rows on are all rebuilt from the above rather than stored as authority.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is a value the engine holds and every frame replaces, so every
field and every array is declared `readonly`: a frame builds the next state from
the current one rather than writing into it, and every reader is handed the state
as `DeepReadonly<MeltdownState>` in any case.

`TowerType`, `SurgeType`, `ModeName`, `DifficultyName`, and `VentName` come from
`src/constants.ts`.

