## The screen and the run

- `screen` — the screen currently shown, one of the eight `specs/screens.md`
  defines.
- `phase` — the sub-phase of the `playing` screen, as `specs/waves.md` defines
  it. It holds `opening` on every other screen.
- `menuIndex` — the highlighted row on whichever menu `screen` is showing,
  counted from `0`. It rests at `0` on `playing`.
- `mode`, `difficulty` — the pair the run is being played on. Every derived
  figure of `specs/modes.md` follows them.
- `money`, `lives`, `score`, `wave` — the run's figures.
- `buildTimer` — the seconds left in the current build phase, counting down
  against the game time each frame advances by, and resting at `0` in the
  `opening` phase.
- `wavePending` — how many units of the current wave are still to be released.
- `spawnClock` — the wave spawner's own release accumulator, in seconds.
- `speed` — the game-speed toggle, `1` or `2`.

## `TowerState`

One tower standing on the floor.

- `id` — distinct among every entity live at that moment, and kept for as long as
  the tower stands.
- `type` — one of the eight of `specs/towers.md`.
- `col`, `row` — the footprint's top-left tile.
- `rotation` — the placement rotation, `0` to `3`, fixed once the tower is
  placed.
- `level` — `1`, `2`, or `3`.
- `heat` — `0` to `100`, and `0` for the Forge and the Sink.
- `tripped`, `tripTimer` — whether the tower is offline on a trip, and the
  seconds left on that cooldown, resting at `0`.
- `fireClock` — the tower's own fire accumulator, in seconds, as
  `specs/combat.md` describes it.
- `targeting` — the id of the unit it is firing on, or `null`.
- `firing` — whether the tower has a target and is online this frame.
- `kills`, `damageDealt` — the tower's own tallies.
- `spent` — everything spent on this tower, its build cost plus every upgrade.
- `fresh` — whether it still refunds in full, as `specs/building.md` states.
- `firingEnabled`, `thermalEnabled` — the two faculty gates the debug surface
  poses. Both are `true` for a tower the game itself placed.

`towers` lists every tower on the floor, and a tower added through the debug
surface or committed by a placement is appended to it.

## `UnitState`

One surge unit on the floor.

- `id` — distinct among every entity live at that moment, and kept for as long as
  the unit exists.
- `type` — one of the six of `specs/surge.md`.
- `x`, `y` — the unit's centre.
- `hp`, `maxHp` — its current and maximum hp.
- `slowFactor` — the fraction of its base speed the live slow removes, `0` for
  none.
- `slowTimer` — the seconds the live slow has left, `0` for none.
- `vent` — the vent it entered at, which fixes its exhaust.
- `motion` — the locomotion gate the debug surface poses. It is `true` for a unit
  the game itself released.

`surge` lists every unit on the floor, and a unit added through the debug surface
is appended to it.

## `BuildState` and `PointerState`

- `build` is the held placement preview, or `null` when none is armed. It carries
  the type held, the footprint's top-left tile, and the held rotation. Whether
  that footprint is placeable is decided from the floor at the read rather than
  stored.
- `pointer` carries the pointer's position, in logical stage units, and whether
  it is currently pressed.

## The rest

- `waveSpawning` — whether the run's own release of surge runs, which the debug
  surface poses.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in
  every update. The runtime owns muting, and this field is what the debug surface
  reports.
- `nextId` — the id the next tower or unit takes, so no two entities live at one
  moment share one.
- `simTime` — accumulated simulation time, in seconds. Every update adds the game
  time it advanced by, whatever the screen.
- `rngState` — the state of the game's seeded random generator. The vent each
  unit enters at is drawn from it.

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- `initialize` builds the whole state in one go, so every field is present by the
  time a frame can observe it, and none of them is optional.
- The state handed to `update` is the whole of the game, and the state `update`
  returns is the whole of the next frame. Every value the game carries from one
  frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level variable or a
  closure.
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<MeltdownState>`, to
  `update`, to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation of
  the debug surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new
  `MeltdownState` built from the one they received; `render`, the sources, and
  `snapshot` return no state.
{{else}}
- The engine builds the state when the world opens, through the mode's
  `gameStateClass`, so every field is present by the time a frame can observe it,
  and none of them is optional.
- The state is the whole of the authoritative game. Every value the game carries
  from one frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level
  variable or a closure, and any field an actor, component, or controller holds
  of its own is derived data rebuilt from the declared ones.
- The state is written only through the paths play runs on: the player controller
  resolves the frame's input into it, the game mode's tick advances it, and the
  debug surface's poses arrange it at the call. Rendering reads it and writes
  nothing.
{{/if}}
- Fields you add hold derived data you can rebuild from the declared ones. The
  run's derived figures, the routes across the floor, and the rectangles the
  build panel laid its controls out on and the current screen drew its menu rows
  on are all of that kind. A `reset` restores
  the declared fields to their title-screen values, so those fields are the whole
  of the authoritative state.
{{/if}}
